{
  "concept": "Two angles of depression from one airplane to two points on a straight road form right triangles that share the same altitude, so tangent ratios and the known spacing between the points link all unknown distances.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "small airplane icon at upper left, nose pointing left",
    "teal dashed horizontal reference line extending rightward from the airplane",
    "solid black horizontal highway line across the lower part of the figure",
    "point A on the highway near the middle, labeled 'A'",
    "point B on the highway farther right, labeled 'B'",
    "gray dashed steep sight line from the airplane to point A",
    "gray dashed shallow sight line from the airplane to point B",
    "angle-of-depression arc from the horizontal reference to the shallow sight line, labeled with its current angle, default 25°",
    "angle-of-depression arc from the horizontal reference to the steep sight line, labeled with its current angle, default 49°",
    "vertical altitude segment from the airplane down to the highway, labeled h when revealed",
    "foot of the altitude on the highway, labeled P when revealed",
    "right-angle marker where the altitude meets the highway",
    "horizontal road-distance segment from P to A, labeled PA or x when revealed",
    "horizontal road-distance segment from A to B, labeled AB or d when revealed",
    "horizontal road-distance segment from P to B, labeled PB = x + d when revealed",
    "slant-distance label from airplane to A, labeled s_A when revealed",
    "slant-distance label from airplane to B, labeled s_B when revealed",
    "equation callouts for tan(theta_A) = h / PA and tan(theta_B) = h / PB"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "airplaneAltitude",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Airplane altitude",
      "range": [
        1,
        6,
        0.1
      ],
      "default": 3,
      "teaches": "Changing altitude shows that both right triangles share the same vertical side, so changing h changes both depression angles through the tangent ratios"
    },
    {
      "id": "distanceAB",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Distance between A and B",
      "range": [
        1,
        8,
        0.1
      ],
      "default": 4,
      "teaches": "Moving B farther from A makes the outer sight line shallower and shows that the larger triangle's horizontal leg is the smaller leg plus AB"
    },
    {
      "id": "setupPhase",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Solve setup phase",
      "states": [
        "horizontalReference",
        "rightTriangles",
        "revealUnknowns"
      ],
      "default": "horizontalReference",
      "teaches": "Stepping through the setup makes clear why the dashed horizontal creates angles of depression, why the altitude creates right triangles, and which distances become the unknowns in the tangent equations"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Draw the horizontal reference",
      "narration": "The dashed teal line represents the airplane's horizontal line of sight. The two labeled angles are measured downward from this horizontal, so they are angles of depression.",
      "state": {
        "airplaneAltitude": 3,
        "distanceAB": 4,
        "setupPhase": "horizontalReference"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Form the shared right triangles",
      "narration": "Dropping a vertical altitude from the airplane to the road creates two right triangles. Both triangles use the same altitude h, but their horizontal legs reach to different road points.",
      "state": {
        "airplaneAltitude": 3,
        "distanceAB": 4,
        "setupPhase": "rightTriangles"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Connect the unknown distances",
      "narration": "If PA is x and AB is d, then PB is x + d. The tangent equations tan(49°) = h/x and tan(25°) = h/(x+d) express the same altitude in two ways.",
      "state": {
        "airplaneAltitude": 3,
        "distanceAB": 4,
        "setupPhase": "revealUnknowns"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Change altitude",
      "narration": "Raise or lower the airplane while A and B stay fixed. The shared altitude changes both triangles at once, so both depression angles update together.",
      "state": {
        "airplaneAltitude": 4.5,
        "distanceAB": 4,
        "setupPhase": "revealUnknowns"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move B along the highway",
      "narration": "Increase the distance from A to B. The line of sight to B becomes shallower, making the outer tangent ratio depend on the added road distance AB.",
      "state": {
        "airplaneAltitude": 3,
        "distanceAB": 6,
        "setupPhase": "revealUnknowns"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The two sight lines create two right triangles with one common altitude, and the horizontal leg to B is the horizontal leg to A plus the known distance AB.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Use a clean side-view SVG diagram. Place the airplane in the upper left and the highway as a solid black horizontal line near the bottom. Keep A between the airplane's vertical foot and B, with B far to the right. Draw the horizontal reference as a teal dashed line from the airplane to the right. Draw both sight lines as gray dashed rays from the airplane to A and B; the A line should be visibly steeper than the B line. Angle arcs sit at the airplane, opening downward from the teal reference; label the shallower arc about 25° and the steeper arc about 49° at the default slider settings. In later phases reveal the vertical altitude to the road, the right-angle marker, the foot point P, distance labels h, x, d, x+d, and tangent-equation callouts. Sliders should smoothly recompute geometry and angle labels while preserving the road as horizontal and A fixed."
}

